<!-- ==================== BANNER: TITLE & AUTHORS (FULL WIDTH) ==================== -->
<div class="SectionTitle">
    <h1 class="paper-title">CampusGrid: An Integrated Framework for Spatial Hierarchy Modeling, Dual-Vector Collision-Free Course Timetabling, and Deterministic Anti-Cheating Examination Seating Allocation</h1>
    
    <div class="author-block">
        <div class="author-names">Nikhil Kumawat<sup>1</sup>, Palak Nandani<sup>1</sup>, Kashish Upadhyay<sup>1</sup>, Priyanshu Kumar<sup>1</sup>, Bhawni Kumari<sup>1</sup>, and Er. Ram Babu Buri<sup>2</sup></div>
        <div class="author-affil"><sup>1</sup>Department of Computer Science & Engineering, Institute of Engineering & Technology</div>
        <div class="author-affil"><sup>2</sup>Assistant Professor & Project Supervisor, Department of Computer Science & Engineering</div>
        <div class="author-email">{nk2423797, palaknlalawat, kashishu314, priyanshu9246, bhawnibhawni393}@gmail.com</div>
    </div>

    <div class="abstract-box">
        <span class="abstract-title">Abstract</span>—Higher education institutions routinely grapple with the complex operational challenge of optimizing physical campus space, scheduling hundreds of overlapping course offerings without collision, and enforcing academic integrity during large-scale examination seating. Historically, these functions are addressed through fragmented enterprise resource planning (ERP) modules, manual spreadsheets, or computationally prohibitive stochastic metaheuristics that lack real-time determinism. This paper presents CampusGrid, an integrated, enterprise-grade software framework that unifies spatial infrastructure modeling, automated conflict-free academic timetabling, and deterministic anti-cheating examination seat allocation into a cohesive, web-native platform. The framework models institutional physical geography through a strict four-tier hierarchical abstraction (Campus &rarr; Block &rarr; Floor &rarr; Room &rarr; Matrix Grid) with real-time seat coordinate state management. To eliminate lecture scheduling clashes, CampusGrid implements a dual-vector temporal-spatial collision detection engine based on closed-interval intersection analysis (<i>S</i><sub>1</sub> &lt; <i>E</i><sub>2</sub> &and; <i>E</i><sub>1</sub> &gt; <i>S</i><sub>2</sub>), guaranteeing zero room, instructor, and student-cohort overlaps in &Omicron;(<i>N</i> log <i>N</i>) time. For examination management, we formulate an alternating bipartite checkerboard seating algorithm that guarantees a Manhattan distance <i>D<sub>M</sub></i>(<i>s<sub>i</sub></i>, <i>s<sub>j</sub></i>) &gt; 1 between candidates sharing identical examinations, eliminating adjacent optical and collaborative cheating pathways. Implemented using Java 17 LTS, Spring Boot 3.3.4, Hibernate/JPA, MySQL 8.0, and an HMAC-SHA512 stateless JWT security layer, CampusGrid was evaluated against synthetic and empirical university workloads encompassing 2,500 students, 50 rooms, and 200 course sections. Experimental benchmarks demonstrate sub-5 millisecond collision verification, linear scaling of exam allocation at &gt;1,200 seats/sec, 100% adherence to anti-cheating distance constraints across 140 automated integration test suites, and sub-15ms HTTP API response latencies under cloud-native containerized deployment on Railway PaaS.<br>
        <div class="index-terms">
            <span class="index-terms-title">Index Terms</span>—Academic Space Management, University Course Timetabling Problem (UCTP), Examination Timetabling Problem (ETP), Anti-Cheating Heuristics, Spatial Hierarchy, Conflict Detection, Bipartite Matching, Graph Coloring, Spring Boot, Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ==================== TWO-COLUMN CONTENT BODY ==================== -->
<div class="SectionTwoCol">

<h2 class="ieee-heading">I. Introduction</h2>

<p class="body-text">Higher education institutions operate as multi-faceted micro-cities, administering dozens of academic departments, hundreds of faculty members, and thousands of matriculated students across dispersed physical infrastructures. Efficiently orchestrating this environment requires solving three interrelated, computationally hard combinatorial problems: (1) dynamically modeling physical classrooms, computer laboratories, lecture halls, and auditoriums with precise capacity, operational state, and coordinate seat layouts; (2) allocating time slots and rooms to hundreds of courses such that no physical room, faculty instructor, or student cohort experiences temporal overlap; and (3) distributing examination candidates across multi-room facilities while strictly enforcing academic integrity by isolating students taking identical papers from physical proximity [1].</p>

<p class="body-text">In conventional university administrative workflows, these domains remain profoundly siloed. Space allocation is frequently managed through static building registries or disconnected facility spreadsheets that fail to communicate with academic scheduling software. Timetabling relies either on manual human scheduling—prone to human error, room double-bookings, and instructor fatigue—or complex, non-deterministic metaheuristics that require prolonged computation times and produce unpredictable schedule reorganizations upon minor constraint changes [2].</p>

<p class="body-text">Furthermore, examination seating is frequently relegated to ad-hoc room door printouts, leading to student confusion, invigilator bottlenecks, and academic dishonesty caused by immediate visual or acoustic proximity between peers sitting for identical assessments [3].</p>

<p class="body-text">To overcome these structural limitations, this paper introduces <b>CampusGrid</b>, an integrated software engineering framework that unifies spatial hierarchy modeling, closed-interval collision-free course timetabling, and deterministic anti-cheating examination seat allocation. CampusGrid is engineered from first principles as a high-throughput, cloud-native enterprise system, combining strict relational integrity, formal mathematical constraint enforcement, and an intuitive black-and-white visual interface for campus administrators and matriculated students [4].</p>

<p class="body-text">The primary contributions of this paper are summarized as follows:</p>
<ul style="margin-top:2pt; margin-bottom:4pt; padding-left:14pt; font-size:9.5pt;">
    <li><b>Formal Four-Tier Spatial Hierarchy Model:</b> A relational and coordinate-based spatial abstraction (Campus &rarr; Block &rarr; Floor &rarr; Room &rarr; Matrix Grid) modeling walkway aisles, podium voids, and workstation facilities deterministically.</li>
    <li><b>Dual-Vector Collision Detection Engine:</b> A swept-line interval conflict algorithm evaluating temporal intervals and spatial-agent dependencies (room, instructor, section) in &Omicron;(<i>N</i> log <i>N</i>) time.</li>
    <li><b>Deterministic Anti-Cheating Seating Allocation Engine:</b> An alternating bipartite checkerboard allocation algorithm proving mathematically that any two candidates taking the same examination maintain a Manhattan distance <i>D<sub>M</sub></i> &gt; 1.</li>
    <li><b>Cloud-Native Architecture with Cryptographic RBAC:</b> A production implementation using Java 17 LTS, Spring Boot 3.3.4, MySQL 8.0, and stateless HMAC-SHA512 JWT containerized via multi-stage Docker builds on Railway.</li>
    <li><b>Extensive Empirical Validation:</b> Rigorous benchmarking across synthetic datasets and 140 automated integration test scenarios, validating sub-5ms operational latency.</li>
</ul>

<h2 class="ieee-heading">II. Related Work & Literature Review</h2>

<p class="body-text">Academic timetabling, facility scheduling, and spatial allocation have been rigorously investigated across operations research, computer science, and institutional management for over four decades. To establish a rigorous foundation, we synthesize the literature into six primary thematic domains encompassing 30 foundational research papers [5].</p>

<h3 class="ieee-subheading">A. Foundational Surveys and Complexity of UCTP and ETP</h3>
<p class="body-text">The academic course timetabling problem was categorized as an &Nu;&Rho;-complete combinatorial optimization problem by Carter and Laporte in their seminal survey, mapping timetabling to classical Graph Vertex Coloring where events constitute vertices and clashes define undirected edges [1].</p>

<p class="body-text">Burke, Elliman, Ford, and Weare conducted extensive surveys of British universities, formalizing the vital distinction between <i>Hard Constraints</i> (physical room availability and double-booking avoidance) and <i>Soft Constraints</i> (student gap minimization and examination spreading) [2].</p>

<p class="body-text">Andrea Schaerf presented an exhaustive mathematical classification of automated timetabling, establishing standard benchmark formulations for school, university course, and examination scheduling [3].</p>

<p class="body-text">Burke and Petrovic explored modern search directions, emphasizing multi-criteria evaluation and case-based reasoning to balance conflicting faculty and institutional objectives [4].</p>

<p class="body-text">Rhyd Lewis published a comprehensive survey of metaheuristic techniques for university timetabling, analyzing simulated annealing, tabu search, and evolutionary algorithms across benchmark datasets [5].</p>

<p class="body-text">Babaei, Karimpour, and Hadidi categorized structural approaches for course timetabling, observing that purely stochastic methods often struggle to guarantee zero hard-constraint violations in production software [6].</p>

<p class="body-text">Recently, Chen, Kendall, and Kendall surveyed state-of-the-art metaheuristics and machine learning in IEEE Access, demonstrating that hybrid architectures combining greedy deterministic heuristics for hard constraints with local search for soft constraints yield optimal practical utility [7].</p>

<h3 class="ieee-subheading">B. Exact Methods and Integer Linear Programming (ILP)</h3>
<p class="body-text">Exact mathematical programming approaches formulate university timetabling as 0–1 Integer Linear Programming (ILP) models. Daskalaki, Birbas, and Housos presented a binary integer programming formulation for university scheduling, creating exact constraint matrices for faculty and room availability [8].</p>

<p class="body-text">MirHassani developed a computational decomposition approach that divided timetabling into time-slot assignments and room allocations, significantly reducing binary decision variable counts [9].</p>

<p class="body-text">Di Gaspero and Schaerf demonstrated that Tabu Search effectively navigates complex neighborhood topologies when initial solution states are populated by deterministic feasibility algorithms [10].</p>

<p class="body-text">Santos, Ochi, and Marinho introduced an integer programming formulation using branch-and-cut column generation for weekly course timetabling, solving real-world Brazilian university instances to global optimality [11].</p>

<p class="body-text">Lach and Lübbecke established multi-stage branch-and-cut algorithms for curriculum-based course timetabling, demonstrating that exact approaches achieve provable global optimality on moderately sized instances but experience exponential runtime degradation when scaling beyond several hundred concurrent sections [12].</p>

<h3 class="ieee-subheading">C. Evolutionary, Genetic, and Memetic Algorithms</h3>
<p class="body-text">Given the computational complexity of exact solvers on large instances, evolutionary computation has been extensively studied. Abdullah, Petrovic, and Burke investigated Variable Neighborhood Search (VNS) coupled with randomized restarts, illustrating how localized neighborhood perturbation avoids entrapment in local minima [13].</p>

<p class="body-text">Nelishia Pillay published a comparative review of objective functions in university examination timetabling, demonstrating that penalty weights assigned to consecutive examinations drastically impact solver convergence [14].</p>

<p class="body-text">Jat and Yang developed a hybrid genetic algorithm integrated with guided Tabu Search, incorporating domain-specific crossover operators to preserve conflict-free chromosomes [15].</p>

<p class="body-text">Beligiannis, Moschopoulos, Karras, and Likothanassis implemented an adaptive genetic algorithm for school timetabling that minimized student and teacher idle hours through specialized mutation operators [16].</p>

<p class="body-text">Yang, Jat, and Burke designed a cellular genetic algorithm with localized search for joint course and examination scheduling in IEEE Transactions on Evolutionary Computation, showing that spatial cellular grid structures enhance diversity across candidate timetable solutions [17].</p>

<h3 class="ieee-subheading">D. Examination Scheduling, Proctoring, and Anti-Cheating Seating Layouts</h3>
<p class="body-text">While standard examination timetabling literature focuses primarily on time-slot clash avoidance, practical examination operations require solving the physical seat assignment problem under strict anti-cheating constraints. Al-Yakoob and Sherali pioneered mathematical programming models for class scheduling and proctor assignments [18].</p>

<p class="body-text">Crucially, Al-Yakoob and Sherali subsequently formalized mathematical programming models specifically enforcing anti-cheating seating protocols, utilizing mixed-integer programming to establish physical separation buffers between students taking identical examination papers [19].</p>

<p class="body-text">Kathryn Dowsland applied Tabu Search to solve practical examination timetabling and desk allocations at a major university, noting that assigning students to specific physical seats was historically done manually due to software deficits [20].</p>

<p class="body-text">Qu, Burke, McCollum, Merlot, and Lee surveyed automated examination systems, identifying spatial separation and seat assignment during examination sittings as a vital, under-researched operational area [21].</p>

<p class="body-text">Asmuni, Burke, Garibaldi, McCollum, and Parkes introduced fuzzy multiple heuristic orderings to prioritize exam assignments based on seat capacity constraints and room availability [22].</p>

<h3 class="ieee-subheading">E. Spatial Facility Planning and Smart Campus Hierarchies</h3>
<p class="body-text">Effective timetabling cannot exist in isolation from campus physical infrastructure. Beyrouthy, Burke, McCollum, and Burke conducted extensive empirical investigations into space utilization in UK universities, revealing that lecture halls and seminar rooms are frequently utilized below 35% of their total potential capacity due to rigid scheduling and lack of visual room modeling [23].</p>

<p class="body-text">Faria, Almeida, and Silva demonstrated in IEEE Access that web-based facility management frameworks and real-time room status tracking drastically improve institutional room utilization rates [24].</p>

<p class="body-text">Min-Allah, Al-Ghamdi, and Al-Hashim demonstrated that campus spatial modeling and automated resource allocation using web-based geographic information systems provide administrators with transparent visibility into underutilized assets [25].</p>

<h3 class="ieee-subheading">F. Enterprise Security, Role-Based Access Control, and Cloud Architecture</h3>
<p class="body-text">Modern educational software requires rigorous, standards-compliant security to prevent unauthorized grade manipulation, schedule tampering, or identity spoofing. Sandhu, Coyne, Feinstein, and Youman formalized the foundational Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) model, establishing clear mathematical mappings between subjects, roles, and resource operations [26].</p>

<p class="body-text">Ferraiolo, Sandhu, Gavrila, Kuhn, and Chandramouli established the proposed NIST standard for role-based access control, formalizing hierarchical role inheritance and constraint separation [27].</p>

<p class="body-text">Bernabe, Canovas, Hernandez-Ramos, Moreno, and Skarmeta expanded RBAC to semantic-aware authorization frameworks for enterprise web portals in IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics and Security [28].</p>

<p class="body-text">In cloud-native educational platforms, stateless authorization is universally governed by RFC 7519 JSON Web Tokens (JWT) secured via HMAC-SHA512 algorithms [29].</p>

<p class="body-text">Transport layer security is governed by RFC 8446 TLS 1.3, guaranteeing end-to-end cryptographic integrity and forward secrecy for all web client-server communications [30].</p>

