<h2 class="ieee-heading">III. System Architecture & Spatial Hierarchy Modeling</h2>

<p class="body-text">The architecture of CampusGrid is structured according to a strict multi-tiered Model-View-Controller (MVC) paradigm, decoupling persistence, transactional domain logic, security interceptors, and presentation layers [26].</p>

<p class="body-text">Physical higher-education space cannot be realistically modeled as a flat table of room strings. CampusGrid defines a formal mathematical tuple for institutional spatial hierarchy [25]:</p>

<div class="equation-box">
    &Sopf; = (&Copf;, &Bopf;, &Fopf;, &Ropf;, &Mopf;)
    <span class="equation-num">(1)</span>
</div>

<p class="body-noindent">Where:</p>
<ul style="margin-top:1pt; margin-bottom:3pt; padding-left:14pt; font-size:9pt;">
    <li>&Copf; = {<i>c</i><sub>1</sub>, <i>c</i><sub>2</sub>, ..., <i>c<sub>p</sub></i>} is the set of physical university campuses.</li>
    <li>&Bopf; = {<i>b</i> | parent(<i>b</i>) &isin; &Copf;} is the set of structural blocks or buildings.</li>
    <li>&Fopf; = {<i>f</i> | parent(<i>f</i>) &isin; &Bopf;} represents floor levels (including subterranean basements).</li>
    <li>&Ropf; = {<i>r</i> | parent(<i>r</i>) &isin; &Fopf;} is the set of rooms, parameterized by room code, functional type &tau;<sub><i>r</i></sub> &isin; {CLASSROOM, LABORATORY, AUDITORIUM, SEMINAR}, capacity <i>K<sub>r</sub></i>, workstation count <i>W<sub>r</sub></i>, and dimensions (<i>N</i><sub>rows</sub> &times; <i>N</i><sub>cols</sub>).</li>
    <li>&Mopf;<sub><i>r</i></sub> &isin; &#8477;<sup><i>N</i><sub>rows</sub> &times; <i>N</i><sub>cols</sub></sup> is the coordinate seating matrix for room <i>r</i>.</li>
</ul>

<p class="body-text">Each coordinate (<i>i</i>, <i>j</i>) in matrix &Mopf;<sub><i>r</i></sub> maps to a discrete physical seat object <i>s</i><sub><i>i</i>, <i>j</i></sub> governed by a four-state deterministic finite automaton: &Sigma;<sub><i>s</i></sub> = {AVAILABLE, OCCUPIED, BLOCKED, MAINTENANCE}. This state machine ensures that structural walkways, pillars, and defective workstations are excluded from operational allocations. The effective allocatable capacity <i>K</i><sub>eff</sub>(<i>r</i>) is defined as [23]:</p>

<div class="equation-box">
    <i>K</i><sub>eff</sub>(<i>r</i>) = &sum;<sub><i>i</i>=1</sub><sup><i>N</i><sub>rows</sub></sup> &sum;<sub><i>j</i>=1</sub><sup><i>N</i><sub>cols</sub></sup> &#120793;(state(<i>s</i><sub><i>i</i>, <i>j</i></sub>) == AVAILABLE)
    <span class="equation-num">(2)</span>
</div>

<h2 class="ieee-heading">IV. Mathematical Modeling & Algorithmic Formulations</h2>

<h3 class="ieee-subheading">A. Dual-Vector Collision-Free Course Timetabling Engine</h3>
<p class="body-text">The University Course Timetabling Problem within CampusGrid requires scheduling an academic class session &Tau;<sub><i>k</i></sub> = (<i>U<sub>k</sub></i>, <i>K<sub>k</sub></i>, <i>I<sub>k</sub></i>, <i>R<sub>k</sub></i>, <i>D<sub>k</sub></i>, <i>S<sub>k</sub></i>, <i>E<sub>k</sub></i>), where <i>U<sub>k</sub></i> is the course subject, <i>K<sub>k</sub></i> is the student cohort, <i>I<sub>k</sub></i> is the faculty instructor, <i>R<sub>k</sub></i> &isin; &Ropf; is the room, <i>D<sub>k</sub></i> is the day of the week, <i>S<sub>k</sub></i> is the start time in minutes, and <i>E<sub>k</sub></i> is the end time [8].</p>

<p class="body-text"><b>Lemma 1 (Closed-Interval Non-Intersection):</b> Two temporal intervals [<i>S<sub>a</sub></i>, <i>E<sub>a</sub></i>) and [<i>S<sub>b</sub></i>, <i>E<sub>b</sub></i>) do not intersect if and only if either <i>E<sub>a</sub></i> &le; <i>S<sub>b</sub></i> or <i>E<sub>b</sub></i> &le; <i>S<sub>a</sub></i>. By De Morgan's laws, they intersect if and only if [9]:</p>

<div class="equation-box">
    Overlap(&Tau;<sub><i>a</i></sub>, &Tau;<sub><i>b</i></sub>) &iff; (<i>S<sub>a</sub></i> &lt; <i>E<sub>b</sub></i>) &and; (<i>E<sub>a</sub></i> &gt; <i>S<sub>b</sub></i>)
    <span class="equation-num">(3)</span>
</div>

<p class="body-text">A candidate schedule &DoubleStruckCapitalT; is feasible if and only if for all pairs &Tau;<sub><i>a</i></sub>, &Tau;<sub><i>b</i></sub> &isin; &DoubleStruckCapitalT; sharing the same day, Overlap(&Tau;<sub><i>a</i></sub>, &Tau;<sub><i>b</i></sub>) implies <i>R<sub>a</sub></i> &ne; <i>R<sub>b</sub></i> (Room Mutex), <i>I<sub>a</sub></i> &ne; <i>I<sub>b</sub></i> (Instructor Mutex), and <i>K<sub>a</sub></i> &ne; <i>K<sub>b</sub></i> (Section Mutex) [11].</p>

<div class="algo-container">
<div class="algo-header">Algorithm 1: Dual-Vector Conflict Resolution Engine</div>
<b>Input:</b> Existing schedule &Tau; = {<i>t</i><sub>1</sub>, ..., <i>t<sub>m</sub></i>}, Candidate <i>t</i>*<br>
<b>Output:</b> Boolean (True if valid; False otherwise), Diagnostics &Delta;<br>
1: <b>Function</b> ValidateAndSchedule(&Tau;, <i>t</i>*):<br>
2: &nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> <i>t</i>*.<i>S</i>* &ge; <i>t</i>*.<i>E</i>* <b>then return</b> (False, ["Invalid Time Interval"])<br>
3: &nbsp;&nbsp;&Tau;<sub>day</sub> &larr; { <i>t</i> &isin; &Tau; | <i>t</i>.Day == <i>t</i>*.<i>D</i>* &and; <i>t</i>.Id &ne; <i>t</i>*.Id }<br>
4: &nbsp;&nbsp;<b>for each</b> <i>t<sub>k</sub></i> &isin; &Tau;<sub>day</sub> <b>do</b><br>
5: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;isOverlapping &larr; (<i>t</i>*.<i>S</i>* &lt; <i>t<sub>k</sub></i>.<i>E</i>) &and; (<i>t</i>*.<i>E</i>* &gt; <i>t<sub>k</sub></i>.<i>S</i>)<br>
6: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> isOverlapping <b>then</b><br>
7: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> <i>t<sub>k</sub></i>.Room == <i>t</i>*.<i>R</i>* <b>then</b> Append(&Delta;, "Room Conflict")<br>
8: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> <i>t<sub>k</sub></i>.Instructor == <i>t</i>*.<i>I</i>* <b>then</b> Append(&Delta;, "Instructor Conflict")<br>
9: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> <i>t<sub>k</sub></i>.Cohort == <i>t</i>*.<i>K</i>* <b>then</b> Append(&Delta;, "Cohort Conflict")<br>
10: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>end if</b><br>
11: &nbsp;&nbsp;<b>end for</b><br>
12: &nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> Length(&Delta;) &gt; 0 <b>then return</b> (False, &Delta;)<br>
13: &nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> SizeOf(<i>t</i>*.<i>K</i>*.Students) &gt; <i>t</i>*.<i>R</i>*.EffectiveCapacity <b>then</b><br>
14: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>return</b> (False, ["Room Capacity Exceeded"])<br>
15: &nbsp;&nbsp;CommitToDatabase(<i>t</i>*)<br>
16: &nbsp;&nbsp;<b>return</b> (True, EmptyVector)<br>
17: <b>End Function</b>
</div>

<p class="body-text">By maintaining composite database B-Tree indexes on (day_of_week, room_id) and (day_of_week, section_id), existing candidate entries are queried in &Omicron;(log <i>M</i>) time, guaranteeing real-time response latency [12].</p>

<h3 class="ieee-subheading">B. Deterministic Anti-Cheating Examination Seating Engine</h3>
<p class="body-text">To prevent academic dishonesty via visual observation or paper exchange during examinations, the seating engine assigns student candidates into coordinate room matrices [18]. We define the Manhattan distance between seats <i>s<sub>a</sub></i> = (<i>r<sub>a</sub></i>, <i>c<sub>a</sub></i>) and <i>s<sub>b</sub></i> = (<i>r<sub>b</sub></i>, <i>c<sub>b</sub></i>) as [19]:</p>

<div class="equation-box">
    <i>D<sub>M</sub></i>(<i>s<sub>a</sub></i>, <i>s<sub>b</sub></i>) = |<i>r<sub>a</sub></i> - <i>r<sub>b</sub></i>| + |<i>c<sub>a</sub></i> - <i>c<sub>b</sub></i>|
    <span class="equation-num">(4)</span>
</div>

<p class="body-text">The system strictly enforces the <b>Adjacency Isolation Invariant</b>, mandating that no two candidates writing the same exam paper sit in immediately adjacent desks: &forall; <i>s<sub>a</sub></i>, <i>s<sub>b</sub></i>, Exam(<i>s<sub>a</sub></i>) = Exam(<i>s<sub>b</sub></i>) &rArr; <i>D<sub>M</sub></i>(<i>s<sub>a</sub></i>, <i>s<sub>b</sub></i>) &gt; 1 [20].</p>

<div class="algo-container">
<div class="algo-header">Algorithm 2: Bipartite Checkerboard Seating Engine</div>
<b>Input:</b> Examination Rooms &Ropf;, Student Cohorts &Qopf; = {<i>Q</i><sub>1</sub>, ..., <i>Q<sub>u</sub></i>}<br>
<b>Output:</b> Mapping &Omega;: Student &rarr; (Room, Row, Col, SeatNumber)<br>
1: <b>Function</b> GenerateAntiCheatingSeating(&Ropf;, &Qopf;):<br>
2: &nbsp;&nbsp;SortedCohorts &larr; SortDescendingByLength(&Qopf;)<br>
3: &nbsp;&nbsp;Cohort_A &larr; SortedCohorts[1]; Cohort_B &larr; Merge(SortedCohorts[2..<i>u</i>])<br>
4: &nbsp;&nbsp;<i>i<sub>A</sub></i> &larr; 0; <i>i<sub>B</sub></i> &larr; 0; &Omega; &larr; EmptyMap()<br>
5: &nbsp;&nbsp;<b>for each</b> room <i>R<sub>m</sub></i> &isin; &Ropf; <b>do</b><br>
6: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>for</b> <i>r</i> &larr; 1 <b>to</b> <i>R<sub>m</sub></i>.Rows <b>do</b><br>
7: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>for</b> <i>c</i> &larr; 1 <b>to</b> <i>R<sub>m</sub></i>.Cols <b>do</b><br>
8: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;seat &larr; <i>R<sub>m</sub></i>.GetSeatAt(<i>r</i>, <i>c</i>)<br>
9: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> seat.State &ne; AVAILABLE <b>then continue</b><br>
10: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;parity &larr; (<i>r</i> + <i>c</i>) mod 2<br>
11: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>if</b> parity == 0 <b>then</b><br>
12: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;target &larr; (<i>i<sub>A</sub></i> &lt; Length(Cohort_A)) ? Cohort_A[<i>i<sub>A</sub></i>++] : Cohort_B[<i>i<sub>B</sub></i>++]<br>
13: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>else</b><br>
14: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;target &larr; (<i>i<sub>B</sub></i> &lt; Length(Cohort_B)) ? Cohort_B[<i>i<sub>B</sub></i>++] : Cohort_A[<i>i<sub>A</sub></i>++]<br>
15: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>end if</b><br>
16: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&Omega;[target] &larr; (<i>R<sub>m</sub></i>, <i>r</i>, <i>c</i>, seat.Number)<br>
17: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;seat.State &larr; OCCUPIED<br>
18: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>end for</b><br>
19: &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>end for</b><br>
20: &nbsp;&nbsp;<b>end for</b><br>
21: &nbsp;&nbsp;BatchPersist(&Omega;); <b>return</b> &Omega;<br>
22: <b>End Function</b>
</div>

<p class="body-text"><b>Theorem 1 (Adjacency Isolation):</b> Let &Gcy; = (<i>V</i>, <i>E</i>) be a grid graph representing room matrix &Mopf;. Under Algorithm 2, for any pair of students assigned to seats <i>v<sub>a</sub></i>, <i>v<sub>b</sub></i> writing identical exams, <i>D<sub>M</sub></i>(<i>v<sub>a</sub></i>, <i>v<sub>b</sub></i>) &ge; 2 &gt; 1.</p>

<p class="body-text"><i>Proof:</i> A 2D lattice grid graph is bipartite. Vertices can be partitioned into independent sets <i>V</i><sub>0</sub> = {(<i>r</i>, <i>c</i>) | (<i>r</i>+<i>c</i>) &equiv; 0 mod 2} and <i>V</i><sub>1</sub> = {(<i>r</i>, <i>c</i>) | (<i>r</i>+<i>c</i>) &equiv; 1 mod 2}. Adjacent vertices share an edge and must differ in parity by &plusmn;1, placing them in opposite sets. Because Algorithm 2 assigns Cohort A candidates to <i>V</i><sub>0</sub> and Cohort B to <i>V</i><sub>1</sub>, students writing the same examination belong to the same independent set. Because no edges exist between vertices in the same independent set, minimum distance is <i>D<sub>M</sub></i> &ge; |1| + |1| = 2 &gt; 1. Direct horizontal and vertical cheating adjacency is mathematically precluded [21], [22]. &FilledSmallSquare;</p>

<h2 class="ieee-heading">V. Implementation, Security & Cloud Deployment</h2>

<p class="body-text">CampusGrid is implemented using Java 17 LTS and Spring Boot 3.3.4, backed by Hibernate 6.4 ORM and MySQL 8.0 with HikariCP connection pooling [28].</p>

<p class="body-text">Security adheres to NIST RBAC standards [27]. Authentication generates stateless HMAC-SHA512 JSON Web Tokens (RFC 7519) carrying encrypted user claims and role authorizations, verified per HTTP request by a custom <code>JwtAuthFilter</code> interceptor [29]. Passwords use BCrypt key derivation with an adaptive cost factor of 10.</p>

<p class="body-text">The system is containerized via a multi-stage Dockerfile: a build layer (<code>maven:3.9.6-eclipse-temurin-17</code>) compiles the application, and a minimal runtime layer (<code>eclipse-temurin:17-jre-alpine</code>) generates a secure 180MB image. Deployed on Railway PaaS, environment variable binding (<code>MYSQLHOST</code>, <code>MYSQLPORT</code>, <code>PORT</code>) dynamically binds internal cloud networks without hardcoded credentials [30].</p>

<h2 class="ieee-heading">VI. Experimental Evaluation & Benchmarks</h2>

<p class="body-text">Empirical benchmarks were executed on an Intel&reg; Core&trade; i5-1135G7 @ 2.40GHz CPU with 16GB DDR4 RAM running Windows 11 Enterprise and OpenJDK 17.0.12 [7].</p>

